"Summary of key findings\n\n- Symptom: 100% ICMP loss between remote-worker 10.1.0.2 and server 10.0.1.2 in both directions (end-to-end reachability failure across branch-fw).\n- Interfaces and routing on branch-fw (ASA) are healthy:\n  - Gi0/0 (outside) 10.0.0.2 up/up; Gi0/1 (inside) 10.0.1.1 up/up.\n  - Routing correct: 10.0.1.0/24 connected on inside; 10.1.0.0/30 learned via OSPF via 10.0.0.1 (outside).\n  - ASA can ping 10.0.1.2 successfully and traceroute to 10.1.0.2 successfully. This rules out L2/L3 link issues on the ASA and upstream toward the branch-router.\n- Access-control is the blocker:\n  - An access-group named ALLOW_INTERSUBNET is applied inbound on both outside and inside.\n  - Packet-tracer shows final action drop (acl-drop) at the outside inbound ACL; hits observed, indicating repeated denials of this exact flow.\n  - A separate packet-tracer from inside shows an implicit deny on the egress path as well, consistent with missing explicit permits when an ACL is applied on that interface.\n  - The ACL entries currently reference wrong subnets (10.0.2.0/24, 10.0.3.0/24, 10.0.4.0/24) and do not reference the actual inside subnet 10.0.1.0/24; no ACE permits traffic between 10.1.0.0/30 and 10.0.1.0/24. Hit counts on existing ACEs are zero.\n- NAT is not implicated (packet-tracer shows use_real_addr, no NAT translation involved).\n- Logging shows no entries for 10.0.1.2, indicating ACL denies are not being logged (implicit deny without an explicit log, or logging not enabled at sufficient level).\n\nPrimary issues and their impact\n\n1) Missing/mismatched ACL permits between 10.1.0.0/30 and 10.0.1.0/24\n- Evidence: packet-tracer acl-drop on outside; zero hits on current ACEs; ACEs reference wrong subnets; bidirectional ICMP loss.\n- Impact: All transit traffic between the remote-worker and server is dropped by the implicit deny, explaining 100% ping loss both ways.\n\n2) Same ACL applied inbound on both outside and inside\n- Evidence: access-group ALLOW_INTERSUBNET in interface outside and in interface inside.\n- Impact:\n  - Doubles the places where traffic can be blocked and requires carefully mirrored ACEs for both directions.\n  - For ICMP, if ICMP inspection is not enabled, echo and echo-reply are separate flows; applying ACLs on both interfaces frequently leads to asymmetric or missing permits, causing drops even when one side is allowed.\n\n3) ACL content does not match actual topology (stale/template ACEs)\n- Evidence: ACL references 10.0.2/10.0.3/10.0.4; no reference to 10.0.1.0/24 or correct pairings with 10.1.0.0/30; zero ACE hit counts.\n- Impact: Legitimate traffic never matches any permit ACE; only the implicit deny is hit, resulting in drops.\n\n4) Insufficient logging for denied flows\n- Evidence: show logging | grep 10.0.1.2 returns no result despite known drops.\n- Impact: Hinders rapid diagnosis; denies are occurring but not visible in logs unless explicit logging is configured or logging level is raised.\n\n5) Default route via management noted (not causal here)\n- Evidence: Gateway of last resort points to management 198.18.1.1.\n- Impact: Not used for 10.0.1.0/24 or 10.1.0.0/30 because specific routes exist; however, if specific routes were lost, traffic might be black-holed toward the management interface. Keep in mind for resiliency/governance.\n\nRemediation recommendations\n\nA. Correct the ACL to match the actual subnets and required flows\n- Replace stale ACEs and add explicit permits between 10.1.0.0/30 and 10.0.1.0/24.\n- If you keep a single outside-inbound ACL (recommended for simplicity) and do not apply an ACL on inside:\n  - Permit ICMP request and reply inbound on outside to cover both directions:\n    - access-list ALLOW_INTERSUBNET extended permit icmp 10.1.0.0 255.255.255.252 10.0.1.0 255.255.255.0 echo\n    - access-list ALLOW_INTERSUBNET extended permit icmp 10.1.0.0 255.255.255.252 10.0.1.0 255.255.255.0 echo-reply\n    - Optionally permit time-exceeded and unreachable for PMTUD/traceroute:\n      - permit icmp any 10.0.1.0 255.255.255.0 time-exceeded\n      - permit icmp any 10.0.1.0 255.255.255.0 unreachable\n- If you must keep ACLs inbound on both outside and inside, mirror the logic appropriately:\n  - Outside ACL must permit 10.1.0.0/30 -> 10.0.1.0/24 (echo, echo-reply, and relevant ICMP types).\n  - Inside ACL must permit 10.0.1.0/24 -> 10.1.0.0/30 (echo, echo-reply, and relevant ICMP types).\n- Prefer using network/service object-groups to avoid typos and maintain intent:\n  - object network OBJ_INSIDE_10_0_1_0 (10.0.1.0/24)\n  - object network OBJ_REMOTE_10_1_0_0_30 (10.1.0.0/30)\n  - access-list ALLOW_INTERSUBNET extended permit icmp object OBJ_REMOTE_10_1_0_0_30 object OBJ_INSIDE_10_0_1_0 echo\n  - access-list ALLOW_INTERSUBNET extended permit icmp object OBJ_REMOTE_10_1_0_0_30 object OBJ_INSIDE_10_0_1_0 echo-reply\n  - And mirrored entries if the inside ACL remains in place.\n\nB. Simplify policy where possible\n- Consider removing the access-group from the inside interface so that inside-to-outside is permitted by security-level (and state) rather than requiring duplicate ACL entries. Keep policy on outside only.\n- Alternatively, enable statefulness for ICMP so you only need to permit the initiating direction:\n  - policy-map global_policy\n    - class inspection_default\n      - inspect icmp\n  - With ICMP inspection, allowing the initiating echo is sufficient; the echo-reply is permitted by inspection state.\n\nC. Enable actionable logging for denies\n- logging enable\n- logging buffered warnings (or informational, depending on volume appetite)\n- Add an explicit catch-all deny with logging at the end of the ACL to surface mismatches during rollback/validation:\n  - access-list ALLOW_INTERSUBNET extended deny ip any any log interval 300\n- Alternatively, add log to specific ACEs you\u2019re validating. Monitor with show logging and show access-list hitcounts.\n\nD. Validate and monitor after changes\n- Use packet trace to confirm allows:\n  - packet-tracer input outside icmp 10.1.0.2 8 0 10.0.1.2\n  - packet-tracer input inside icmp 10.0.1.2 8 0 10.1.0.2\n- Verify ACE hitcounts increase:\n  - show access-list ALLOW_INTERSUBNET\n- Live tests:\n  - Ping from 10.1.0.2 to 10.0.1.2 and vice versa; confirm success.\n  - If ICMP inspection is enabled, verify it is taking effect:\n    - show service-policy | include icmp\n\nE. Governance/cleanup\n- Remove or correct stale ACL entries referring to 10.0.2/10.0.3/10.0.4 to avoid confusion.\n- Review the default route via management. Not causal here, but ensure it cannot become an unintended fallback for data traffic. If the management interface is management-only, document that and ensure desired default routing for data-plane is present (e.g., via outside or dynamic routing).\n\nWhy these steps resolve the issue\n- They introduce explicit, correctly scoped permits for the actual inside and remote subnets where none currently exist, eliminating the implicit deny drops proven by packet-tracer.\n- They reduce policy asymmetry and ICMP-specific pitfalls (by either avoiding a second ACL on inside or enabling ICMP inspection), preventing future bidirectional ping failures.\n- They enable visibility so any residual or future mismatches are immediately observable in logs and hitcounts."